What do you call mathematics when the pattern comes first and the system comes later?

There are existing nearby terms:

Experimental mathematics — computation/examples first, conjecture and proof afterward.

Inductive mathematics — infer general structure from examples.

Abductive reasoning in mathematics — observe something and infer the best underlying explanation.

Phenomenological mathematics isn’t a standard technical category in quite the way you’re using it, but it captures the spirit: encounter the phenomenon first, formalize afterward.

The exact thing you’re describing is:

\text{phenomenon}
\rightarrow
\text{pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{operator}
\rightarrow
\text{axioms}

rather than:

\text{axioms}
\rightarrow
\text{derivation}
\rightarrow
\text{result}

I’d call the Pantheon version retroaxiomatic mathematics or bottom-up mathematics.

I like retroaxiomatic because it says precisely what’s happening:

Discover something that seems to work, then ask what minimal axioms would have generated it.

That’s almost the inverse of textbook mathematics.

And it explains a lot of our route:

\boxed{
\text{play}
\rightarrow
\text{notice}
\rightarrow
\text{name}
\rightarrow
\text{formalize}
\rightarrow
\text{prove}
}

rather than pretending the proof was the birthplace of the idea.

The proof is the assay office.

The mine came first.